================================================================================
WHY CC BY-SA 4.0 (NOT CC BY-NC-SA)
================================================================================

Earlier revisions of Journal Atlas used CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 for content. As of
the v0.x-license-upgrade-cc-by-sa milestone (2026-05), we have upgraded to
CC BY-SA 4.0 to align with libre/open standards:

  - CC BY-SA 4.0 is on Creative Commons' Free Cultural Works whitelist;
    CC BY-NC-SA is not.
  - The NonCommercial clause conflicts with FSF Four Freedoms (freedom 0:
    "any purpose") and OSI Open Source Definition (clause 6: no
    field-of-endeavor restrictions).
  - ShareAlike (copyleft) still protects the commons: any derivative work,
    commercial or non-commercial, must be released under the same license.
    Closed-source absorption of Journal Atlas into proprietary products
    remains prohibited by the copyleft mechanism.

This change broadens compatibility with libre-aligned funding programs
(e.g., NGI Zero Commons Fund, CZI Essential Open Source Software, Wellcome
Open Research) and aligns with the open-knowledge tradition of Wikipedia
(also CC BY-SA).

